detailedExplanation.whatItIs:
A foreign key constraint establishes a parent-child relationship between tables. For example orders.customer_id is a foreign key referencing customers.id, ensuring every order belongs to a real customer. Example:
CREATE TABLE orders (
  id INT PRIMARY KEY,
  customer_id INT REFERENCES customers(id)
);

Constraints are declarative rules attached to columns or tables. NOT NULL bans missing values, UNIQUE ensures no two rows share a value, CHECK validates a boolean condition per row, and DEFAULT provides a fallback value. Example:
CREATE TABLE users (
  email TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE,
  age INT CHECK (age >= 0),
  status TEXT DEFAULT ''active''
);
